<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Background<span id="abstract1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Large language models (LLMs) show promise in medicine, but their deployment in hospitals is limited by restricted access to electronic health record (EHR) systems. The Model Context Protocol (MCP) enables integration between LLMs and external tools.</span></span></p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Objective<span id="abstract1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
To evaluate whether an LLM connected to an EHR database via MCP can autonomously retrieve clinically relevant information in a real hospital setting.</span></span></p>
    
<p id="abstract1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Methods<span id="abstract1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
We developed </span>EHR-MCP<span id="abstract1.3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">, a framework of custom MCP tools integrated with the hospital EHR database, and used GPT-4.1 through a LangGraph ReAct agent to interact with it. Six tasks were tested, derived from use cases of the infection control team (ICT). Eight patients discussed at ICT conferences were retrospectively analyzed. Agreement with physician-generated gold standards was measured.</span></span></p>
    
<p id="abstract1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Results<span id="abstract1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
The LLM consistently selected and executed the correct MCP tools. Except for two tasks, all tasks achieved near-perfect accuracy. Performance was lower in the complex task requiring time-dependent calculations. Most errors arose from incorrect arguments or misinterpretation of tool results. Responses from EHR-MCP were reliable, though long and repetitive data risked exceeding the context window.</span></span></p>
    
<p id="abstract1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Conclusions<span id="abstract1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
LLMs can retrieve clinical data from an EHR via MCP tools in a real hospital setting, achieving near-perfect performance in simple tasks while highlighting challenges in complex ones. EHR-MCP provides an infrastructure for secure, consistent data access and may serve as a foundation for hospital AI agents. Future work should extend beyond retrieval to reasoning, generation, and clinical impact assessment, paving the way for effective integration of generative AI into clinical practice.</span></span></p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>This preprint reports ongoing research with preliminary results and has not been peer-reviewed. The results should be interpreted with caution.</span></span></span>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15957v1/figures/overview.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/209;" width="381" height="209" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S0.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of the EHR-MCP framework.
<br class="ltx_break">The EHR-MCP framework allows large language models (LLMs) to access hospital electronic health record (EHR) data via the Model Context Protocol (MCP). The LLM client first lists available MCP tools and sends a prompt to the cloud-based model (GPT-4.1). The LLM then selects an appropriate tool and issues a call. MCP retrieves and formats the requested clinical data from the hospital information system (HIS) and returns it to the LLM. The model interprets the response and may issue additional calls until it generates a final answer to the initial prompt.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Language models in medicine</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) are generative AI systems trained on vast corpora of text, capable of rapidly comprehending complex inputs and generating coherent responses. Their development has progressed rapidly across diverse domains, including business, science, and software engineering. In medicine, numerous studies have reported potential clinical applications of LLMs<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Examples include generating clinical documents to reduce the burden<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, simplifying medical records into patient-friendly language to enhance understanding<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, and even outperforming physicians in clinical reasoning<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. While LLMs demonstrate strong reasoning and generative performance in benchmark tasks, several challenges remain before they can be applied effectively in real-world clinical environments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>The challenge of prompting</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A major obstacle is how to provide LLMs with the breadth and depth of medical information necessary for reliable clinical reasoning. Because LLMs cannot access information beyond what is explicitly supplied, missing data can markedly degrade their performance. Moreover, lacking robust metacognitive capabilities, LLMs may fail to recognize when essential information is absent, and instead generate plausible but incorrect outputs with unwarranted confidence<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, a phenomenon known as hallucination<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Extracting clinically relevant information from hospital electronic health record (EHR) systems is itself a nontrivial task for both humans and LLMs. This difficulty arises from the complexity of hospital information systems (HIS), in which multiple subsystems operate concurrently and patient data are dispersed across platforms. For instance, at Infection Control Team (ICT) meetings, assessing the appropriate use of antibiotics requires reviewing patient vital signs, lab results, microbiological culture and susceptibility data, and prior antibiotic exposure. These tasks demand the integration of information from multiple systems. Such processes impose a substantial burden, whether performed by clinicians or delegated to LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Most existing studies on medical LLMs have evaluated their generative capabilities under the assumption that all requisite information is already curated and supplied in the prompt. This overlooks the practical challenge of information retrieval. In practice, if clinicians must manually collect and format relevant data for each query, the reduction in workload will be minimal. We argue that the most effective paradigm for clinical AI lies in mirroring clinical reasoning: dynamically selecting and applying appropriate tools to iteratively gather and integrate task-specific information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>AI agents</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, the concept of the ”AI agent” has emerged, referring to systems in which LLMs not only process text but also autonomously invoke external tools to retrieve information and perform actions. AI agents alternate between reasoning and acting, selecting appropriate tools to address user queries<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. For example, when asked about antimicrobial selection, such an agent could automatically gather and integrate data from clinical notes, laboratory results, microbiological cultures, and medication records. Beyond text generation, the deployment of AI agents requires additional capabilities such as reasoning, memory, planning, and reflection, for which decomposition-based evaluation methods have been proposed<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As a framework for coordinating LLMs with external tools, the Model Context Protocol (MCP), introduced by Anthropic, is rapidly becoming the de facto standard<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. MCP provides a unified interface that reduces the rising costs of tool-specific integration and is now being adopted by multiple LLM providers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Besides using external tools, another strategy for automatically extracting relevant information is retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. RAG encodes documents into a vector space, retrieves semantically related passages, and uses them in generation. While effective for unstructured text such as clinical notes, RAG is less suitable for structured numerical data, such as laboratory values, because embedding models often fail to preserve quantitative magnitudes and unit distinctions<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Moreover, this approach differs fundamentally from how clinicians collect information, potentially limiting its compatibility with human–AI collaboration. Of note, RAG and AI agents are not mutually exclusive; in practice, one can use both RAG and AI agents<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.4 </span>Related work</h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Several studies have explored linking LLMs to EHRs through tool integration, similar to the present work. For instance, Ehtesham et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> developed a smartphone-based application linked to the EHR, in which an AI chatbot interacted with patients and providers to support clinical decisions. This approach showed potential but lacked quantitative evaluation. MedAgentBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> provides a more systematic framework, incorporating both retrieval and action-oriented tasks, achieving high accuracy in information retrieval, but its experiments were restricted to simulated environments rather than real-world EHRs. EHRAgent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> adopts an extensible strategy by enabling LLMs to generate SQL queries, yet its retrieval success rate remains low, posing substantial challenges for clinical adoption.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, our study conducted quantitative evaluation. We implemented commonly used clinical information retrieval tasks as deterministic tools, thereby eliminating procedural errors and demonstrating high accuracy. Furthermore, whereas most prior studies have focused only on English-language clinical settings, investigations in non-English contexts remain scarce; our study addresses this gap.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.5 </span>Objectives</h3>

<div id="S1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The objective of this study is to evaluate whether an LLM can autonomously retrieve and apply clinically relevant information using MCP tools integrated with EHR (<span id="S1.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EHR-MCP</span>) in a real-world hospital environment. Specifically, we aim to assess the accuracy of MCP-based information retrieval in supporting ICT activities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The main contributions of this study are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quantitative evaluation of tool-use capability</span>: We quantitatively assessed the LLM<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\CJK@punctchar</span><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\CJK@uniPunct</span>0”80”99s capability to invoke external tools, distinguishing this function from text generation and reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Real-world integration of MCP</span>: We implemented and deployed EHR-MCP within an operational hospital system, allowing real-world deployment and evaluation while ensuring adaptability to diverse clinical research settings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-linguistic validation</span>: By validating performance in both English and Japanese, we provide evidence that MCP tools can support medical AI agents across varied clinical contexts worldwide.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Methods</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Ethics statement</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study was approved by the Ethics Committee of Keio University School of Medicine (Approval No. 20241132) and conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki and the Japanese ”Ethical Guidelines for Medical and Biological Research Involving Human Subjects.” Because this was a retrospective study with no direct impact on patients, informed consent was waived under an opt-out policy. All interactions with the LLM were performed through a secure VPN under contractual agreements ensuring that input data would not be used for model training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>System architecture</h3>

<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S2.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.15957v1/figures/swarm_simple_tasks.png" id="S2.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:340/157;" width="340" height="157" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S2.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.15957v1/figures/swarm_complex_tasks.png" id="S2.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:152/157;" width="152" height="157" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance (accuracy or Dice score) for each task. (a) Simple tasks, (b) Complex tasks.
<br class="ltx_break">Each dot represents a patient. For example, in the <span id="S2.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lab_data</span> task, seven patients achieved correct retrievals in all 10 trials, while one patient succeeded in only 5 of 10. A green check mark denotes tasks that reached 100% accuracy across all patients and trials.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ EHR-MCP: Real-world Evaluation of Clinical Information Retrieval by Large Language Models via Model Context Protocol" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides an overview of the EHR-MCP framework. For the detailed system architecture, see Figure <a href="#A1.F1" title="Figure S1 ‣ Appendix A Technical Details ‣ EHR-MCP: Real-world Evaluation of Clinical Information Retrieval by Large Language Models via Model Context Protocol" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S1</span></a>. Data from the hospital<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\CJK@punctchar</span><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\CJK@uniPunct</span>0”80”99s electronic health record (EHR) system were synchronized every 24 hours with the in-hospital data warehouse (DWH). The hospital network was securely connected to the Azure OpenAI Service via a VPN through LiteLLM<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, an in-hospital proxy for LLM connections, and GPT-4.1 was employed. A LangGraph ReAct agent<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> was used, with structured outputs enforcing JSON-formatted responses. MCP tools were implemented in Python using FastMCP<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> (Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.2 System architecture ‣ 2 Methods ‣ EHR-MCP: Real-world Evaluation of Clinical Information Retrieval by Large Language Models via Model Context Protocol" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), and data retrieval from the DWH was performed through SQL queries.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S2.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Implemented MCP Tools</span></figcaption>
<table id="S2.T1.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
