<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The primary evaluation metric was accuracy, defined as the proportion of tasks in which the tool call succeeded and the final output exactly matched the gold standard. For list-based tasks (<span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">culture_history</span> and <span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">antibiotics</span>), agreement was measured using the Dice coefficient. Each prompt was executed 10 times to assess stability and reproducibility, and the mean performance across runs was reported.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For error analysis, execution logs were reviewed. These logs included the initial prompt, the invoked tool and its arguments, the tool responses, and the final output. Errors were manually classified into four categories:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tool Invocation Error: Failure in invoking the appropriate MCP tool.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Argument Error: Incorrect or inappropriate arguments passed to the tool.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Interpretation Error: Misinterpretation of the tool response by the LLM.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Output Format Error: Output not conforming to the specified JSON schema.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Eight cases were evaluated across six tasks. In the <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">calculate_ccr</span> task, one patient undergoing dialysis was excluded, as dosing regimens for dialysis patients are generally determined independently of creatinine clearance. In the <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">culture_neg_abx</span> task, one case was excluded because no vancomycin administration occurred during the data retrieval period.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Task performance is summarized in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.2 System architecture ‣ 2 Methods ‣ EHR-MCP: Real-world Evaluation of Clinical Information Retrieval by Large Language Models via Model Context Protocol" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Correct outputs were obtained in all cases for <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">body_weight</span>, <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">culture_history_species</span>, and <span id="S3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">antibiotics</span>. In <span id="S3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lab_data</span>, one patient yielded incorrect results in 5 of 10 runs. In <span id="S3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">culture_history_detection</span>, one case produced outputs that inappropriately included non-blood culture results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Among the complex tasks, <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">calculate_ccr</span> achieved correct results in all but one case, whereas in <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">culture_neg_abx</span>, some cases produced incorrect outputs in all 10 runs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Analysis of execution logs showed that the LLM consistently invoked the expected tools with appropriate arguments across all tasks. No Tool Invocation Errors or Output Format Errors were observed. Because EHR-MCP operates deterministically, correct arguments always produced correct responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By contrast, errors arose from Argument Errors and Interpretation Errors. For example, in <span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lab_data</span> and <span id="S3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">calculate_ccr</span>, the model sometimes failed to restrict retrieval to the most recent results, instead using an overly broad range that exceeded the record limit, or conversely, narrowing the retrieval strictly to the intervention day and omitting relevant information. As examples of misinterpretation, <span id="S3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">culture_history</span> occasionally included non-blood culture results, and in <span id="S3.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">culture_neg_abx</span>, the model misinterpreted tool outputs in the multi-step process, leading to errors in identifying negative culture dates, antibiotic discontinuation, and the day counts between them.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Principal findings</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study demonstrated that LLMs can effectively use MCP-based tools integrated with an EHR to autonomously retrieve clinically relevant information for infectious disease management. Simple tasks requiring a single tool call achieved near-perfect accuracy, whereas errors were more frequent in the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">culture_neg_abx</span> task, highlighting challenges that must be addressed for practical deployment of MCP in clinical settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Error analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tool Invocation Errors were not observed, indicating that GPT-4.1 consistently selected appropriate tools through MCP. Similarly, structured outputs prevented Output Format Errors, and no functional failures occurred in EHR-MCP execution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Most errors arose from Argument Errors and Interpretation Errors. The former typically involved inappropriate specification of the data retrieval window when not explicitly defined. The latter occurred when MCP responses were lengthy or complex, making it difficult for the LLM to extract the intended information. For example, errors were observed when restricting retrieval to blood culture results or when identifying the most recent entry in an antibiotic administration history. These findings indicate that failures mainly occurred at the stages of argument specification and response interpretation. The adverse effects of such errors might be mitigated by interface design strategies informed by human–computer interaction research. For example, increasing the transparency of tool inputs and raw outputs could facilitate human verification and reduce the likelihood of erroneous information entering clinical decision-making.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Implementation challenges</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although MCP tools executed without errors, several implementation issues emerged, highlighting opportunities to improve integration with LLMs. A major challenge is the length of MCP responses: repeated records such as infusions and prescriptions increase with the retrieval window, occasionally exceeding the context capacity of GPT-4. This problem was mitigated after transitioning to GPT-4.1 with an extended context window, but overly long inputs remain undesirable, as they raise API costs and may degrade response quality<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. To mitigate this, retrieval windows should ideally be explicitly defined in the prompt. However, in tasks such as <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lab_data</span>, the LLM must infer the period to obtain the most recent data, which can lead to errors. Streamlining MCP responses or using auxiliary agents to condense prescription and infusion records within a multi-agent framework may help address these challenges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Hallucinations were also observed when MCP tools were unavailable or when required information was missing. In some cases, the LLM recognized such failures and returned error messages in JSON format; in others, it produced fabricated outputs. For example, when antibiotic data were unavailable, the model incorrectly reported <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\CJK@punctchar</span><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\CJK@uniPunct</span>0”80”9CCTRX<span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\CJK@punctchar</span><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\CJK@uniPunct</span>0”80”9D and <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\CJK@punctchar</span><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\CJK@uniPunct</span>0”80”9CVCM<span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\CJK@punctchar</span><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\CJK@uniPunct</span>0”80”9D from the prompt examples as administered (see Supplementary for the execution log). Adding explicit error-handling instructions to prompts may reduce such errors, but anticipating all possible failure scenarios is difficult. Unforeseen failures may still result in fabricated outputs being presented without user awareness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Limitations and future directions</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study was conducted at a single institution, which limits generalizability. Although the EHR-MCP implementation should be adaptable across institutions by accommodating local system differences at the MCP layer, this has not yet been empirically demonstrated. The sample size and number of tasks were also limited. Future work should examine more diverse and complex tasks with expanded error analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, this study focused exclusively on tool use capability, while advanced reasoning and generation were beyond its scope. Tool use is foundational for clinical AI agents, but effective deployment will also require robust reasoning and domain knowledge. Just as proficiency in EHR navigation alone does not make a clinician competent without diagnostic reasoning and documentation skills, accurate tool use alone is insufficient. Having established high tool-use accuracy in real-world settings, future research should expand to comprehensive evaluations encompassing reasoning and generation, tailored to specific specialties and diseases. In particular, we plan to investigate whether MCP-retrieved information can support decision-making in ICT.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Towards a hospital-wide AI agent infrastructure</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The EHR-MCP implemented in this study represents a reusable AI infrastructure to support a wide range of LLM-based clinical applications. Our ultimate goal extends beyond evaluating tool use capability: by embedding EHR-MCP into hospital operations, we aim to enable rapid, cross-departmental deployment of generative AI projects, thereby accelerating both research and clinical utilization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Demonstrating the clinical utility of generative AI will require rigorous studies in real-world settings. While many current medical LLM studies rely on retrospective analyses of case series or datasets, future research must demonstrate practical benefits in real-world settings, such as improving patient outcomes and reducing workload. Such studies will likely need to be tailored to specific specialties and diseases; yet, certain challenges will remain common across domains, including secure use of external LLMs under strict privacy protections and efficient retrieval of EHR data. By establishing a shared infrastructure to address these common needs, hospitals can guarantee reliable data access and security, eliminate redundant efforts, and allow research groups to concentrate on domain-specific studies.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study demonstrated that LLMs can effectively use MCP-based tools integrated with an EHR to autonomously retrieve clinically relevant information for infectious disease management. By quantitatively evaluating tool use in real-world settings, we showed that simple retrieval tasks can be performed with near-perfect accuracy, while complex tasks revealed challenges related to argument specification and interpretation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">EHR-MCP represents a reusable institutional infrastructure that ensures consistent and secure access to clinical data, thereby reducing redundancy and enabling research groups to focus on advancing clinical and scientific investigations. Building on the high accuracy of tool use, future work should extend evaluations to reasoning and generation, as well as prospective studies assessing the clinical impact of LLM-based systems on outcomes and workflow efficiency. Through such efforts, hospital-wide AI agent infrastructures may help accelerate the safe and effective integration of generative AI into clinical practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
